<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<full-backup-content>
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="flick_pairings.xml" />
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="flick_subtitles_online.xml" />
    <!-- Hashed checkpoints still name MediaStore revisions that exist only on this phone. -->
    <exclude domain="file" path="datastore/flick_playback_progress.preferences_pb" />
    <!-- Remembered A/V nudges are keyed by that same hash, so they name nothing elsewhere. -->
    <exclude domain="file" path="datastore/flick_audio_delay.preferences_pb" />
    <!--
      The remembered subtitles, and the copies those records name. Two reasons, and the
      second is the one that would not be obvious: the records are keyed by the same hash
      of a MediaStore revision that exists only on this phone, so restored they can match
      nothing — and the directory holds verbatim copies of the user's own subtitle files,
      which are their reading of the films they watch and have no business in a cloud.
    -->
    <exclude domain="file" path="datastore/flick_subtitle_memory.preferences_pb" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="subtitle_memory" />
    <!--
      The library scope names a folder of THIS phone's storage. Since the chooser became a
      tree it is written as a normalised RELATIVE_PATH — "Movies/Marvel" — with the older
      MediaStore bucket id only ever read back, never written.

      A path like that IS meaningful on another phone, and that is the reason to leave it
      behind rather than an argument for carrying it. Restored, it does one of two things,
      and the second is worse than the first: it matches nothing, and the library opens on
      the card saying that folder is not there any more; or it matches a folder of the same
      name holding entirely different films, and the library opens silently narrowed to a
      scope its owner never chose on this device — with only a chip naming the folder to
      say so. A legacy bucket id, being a hash of a path on the volume it was read from,
      simply names nothing anywhere else. Neither form survives the move usefully.

      The appearance preference is deliberately NOT here, for this reason reversed: a
      light/dark choice is about the person rather than about one device's storage, and it
      should survive the move.
    -->
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="flick_library.xml" />
</full-backup-content>
